ly  therefore the same principles that apply  to stuff like flowers and bottles and  all that jazz don't apply to the bee so  that means it's like this immovable box  like nothing will push it but that cart  is coming  and it also wants to go this way so it  wants to go this way and the bee is just  minding its own damn business  and so the only way for this to sort of  resolve with physics as it continues to  try and push further and further with  that card because it thinks it's further  it just kind of vibrates and well it has  to go up that's the only way and so and  then  rockets up into the sky that's what  ended up breaking the whole game was  like a literal bug caused the bug  and skyrim would break all the time in  all sorts of ways like that oh it sure  does even to this day even the card  scene is still a little rocky uh which i  could tell you firsthand from trying to  capture it for this very video  [Music]  no this can't be happening this isn't  happening while all of skyrim's problems  haven't been fixed the b culprit had  been found by qa and squashed  [Music]  sort of  because the b can actually still push  you around and i had no idea just look  at this dude get it all up in lizard  wizard's face  [Music]  now even despite all of skyrim's  existing bugs i have still put hundreds  of hours into that game i'd even go so  far to say that i think they've even  grown infinity towards that game's  jankiness like to me it's kind of become  a defining characteristic of the game  experience like a mole or a blemish that  you just kind of accepted as a beauty  mark it doesn't really ruin the game for  me except that be  but really it's incredible to think back  on 10 years of this game and kind of see  how it stepped above its wonkiness and  not only just become a defining part of  my gaming identity but also kind of set  the path for games going forward like  we're now coming on a decade  of its existence and not including the  time it took to develop  what is that like my curiosity it had a  great impact on game so it's clear to me  that like it changed the course of games  in a lot of ways like i talked to people  that worked on like the witcher and  stuff and they were big fans of the game  you can see the influences there so it's  fun to see that impact because i always  liked  personally building games that felt more  like a world and not just a game and i  think a lot of things have shifted  towards that more and it sounds really  obvious now saying 10 years later but  at the time most games were not like  that and of course the memes because  there's always memes the meme stuff  definitely caught us off guard so it was  pretty funny to see the random things  that people would pick up on in the  games like the arrow and the knee thing  is  obviously my favorite because it's just  so dumb and nobody thought twice about  it even though we like played hundreds  of hours you just kind of like  tune it out  so you don't even pick up that okay like  it's kind of a weird thing to say not  expecting people years later to get like  tattoos of arrows on their knees and  stuff i'm still playing skyrim to this  day uh so if that means anything like  from from its launch even on the ps3 now  to mostly have my nintendo switch even  10 years later this game remains like  comfort food to me it's like it's the  thing i play in between other games or  the thing that's permanently on my  nintendo switch when i go on a flight or  like even the bedside nightcap to like  drift off to  a lot of bugs remain in the game but its  environment tone and magical world has  endured and as a place i revisit often  even if just for a walk  as for its opening every time i see it i  know it means the beginning of a new  adventure through northern tamriel  thankfully for nate well he doesn't have  to look at that intro anymore because  he's moved on to pursue game development  as a solo indie dev instead i think  every game to do blooper reel i think  would be the best thing ever i'm keeping  videos on my current game of terrible  bloopers i just know that every time i  see it again or every time i find myself  taking a walk outside the ruins of  helgen that not only will i think about  the developers who had to sit through it  hundreds of times but that almost didn't  happen entirely because  of a b  hey you  you're finally awake  you were trying to cross the border  right  walk right into that imperial ambush  same as us and that thief over there  damn you stormcloaks

